    "explanationEn": "Determine the conductivity from the resistivity. The resistivity is given as ρ = 870.0 m Ω ⋅ m . Convert this into ohm·meters: 870.0 m Ω ⋅ m = 870.0 × 10 − 3 Ω ⋅ m = 0.87 Ω ⋅ m . Conductivity is the reciprocal of resistivity: κ = 1 ρ = 1 0.87 ≈ 1.15 S/m . Find the concentration of NaOH in mol/m³. A 0.2 % ( w / v ) solution means there are 0.2 grams of NaOH per 100 mL of solution. In 1 liter (1000 mL) there are: 0.2 g 100 mL × 1000 mL = 2 g/L . The molar mass of NaOH is approximately 40 g/mol . Thus, the molarity is: Molarity = 2 g/L 40 g/mol = 0.05 mol/L . Since 1 L = 0.001 m 3 , converting to SI units (mol/m³): c = 0.05 mol/L × 1 0.001 m 3 / L = 50 mol/m 3 . Calculate the molar conductivity in SI units. Molar conductivity Λ m is given by: Λ m = κ c . Substituting the values: Λ m = 1.15 S/m 50 mol/m 3 = 0.023 S ⋅ m 2 / mol . Convert the molar conductivity to the desired units (mS dm² mol⁻¹). First, convert the conductivity: 0.023 S ⋅ m 2 / mol × 1000 mS S = 23 mS ⋅ m 2 / mol . Now, convert the area units. Recall that: 1 m 2 = 100 dm 2 . So, 23 mS ⋅ m 2 / mol = 23 × 100 mS ⋅ dm 2 / mol = 2300 mS ⋅ dm 2 / mol . The problem asks for the answer in the form [blank] × 10 2 mS ⋅ dm 2 / mol . Expressing 2300 mS dm²/mol in this form: 2300 mS ⋅ dm 2 / mol = 23 × 10 2 mS ⋅ dm 2 / mol . Thus, the molar conductivity is 23 × 10 2 mS ⋅ dm 2 / mol .",
