    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Derivation of the de Broglie wavelength after time t The de Broglie wavelength is given by λ = h p , where h is Planck's constant and p is the momentum of the particle. Initial Conditions: Initially, the electron has a velocity v ⃗ = v 0 i ˆ , so its momentum is p 0 = m v 0 . The initial de Broglie wavelength is therefore λ 0 = h m v 0 . Effect of the Electric Field: The electron enters an electric field E ⃗ = − E 0 k ˆ . For an electron with charge − e , the force is given by F ⃗ = − e E ⃗ = − e ( − E 0 k ˆ ) = e E 0 k ˆ . This force causes an acceleration in the k ˆ direction: a z = F z m = e E 0 m . Momentum Components After Time t : In the i ˆ direction, there is no force, so the momentum remains constant: p x = m v 0 . In the k ˆ direction, starting from rest, the momentum becomes: p z = m v z = m ( a z t ) = e E 0 t . Total Momentum of the Electron: The total momentum is the vector sum of the components: p = √ p 2 x + p 2 z = √ ( m v 0 ) 2 + ( e E 0 t ) 2 . New de Broglie Wavelength: Substituting the expression for the total momentum into the de Broglie relation gives: λ = h √ m 2 v 2 0 + e 2 E 2 0 t 2 . Expressing this in terms of the initial de Broglie wavelength λ 0 = h m v 0 : λ = λ 0 √ 1 + e 2 E 2 0 t 2 m 2 v 2 0 . This final expression matches the option: λ 0 √ 1 + e 2 E 2 0 t 2 m 2 v 2 0 .",
