    "explanationEn": "When a spherical object falls through a viscous fluid, it eventually reaches a constant speed called terminal velocity ( v t ) . This occurs when the downward force of gravity is balanced by the upward buoyant force and the viscous drag force. The force of gravity acting on the sphere is F g = m g = 4 3 π r 3 ρ g , where ρ is the density of the liquid. Using Archimedes' principle of buoyant force F b = 4 3 π r 3 σ g , where σ is the density of the gas. Using Stokes' Law the viscous drag is F v = 6 π η r v t At terminal velocity, F v = F g − F b ⇒ 6 π η r v t = 4 3 π r 3 ( ρ − σ ) g ⇒ v t = 2 r 2 ( ρ − σ ) g 9 η For the same liquid and gas, terminal velocity is proportional to the square of the radius : v ∝ r 2 The large drop of radius R is broken into 64 identical droplets of radius r . Since the total volume remains constant : V R = 64 × V r ⇒ 4 3 π R 3 = 64 × 4 3 π r 3 ⇒ R 3 = 64 r 3 ⇒ R = 4 r ⇒ r = R 4 Let v 1 be the terminal velocity of the large drop and v 2 be the terminal velocity of a small droplet. Using the relationship v ∝ r 2 : v 1 v 2 = R 2 r 2 Substituting r = R 4 : v 1 v 2 = R 2 ( R 4 ) 2 ⇒ v 1 v 2 = R 2 R 2 / 16 ⇒ v 1 v 2 = 16 Therefore, the ratio of terminal velocities v 1 v 2 is 16 . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
