    "explanationEn": "When unpolarized light of intensity I 0 passes through a polarizer, the intensity of the transmitted polarized light ( I 1 ) is always half of the initial intensity. I 1 = I 0 2 An optically active solution rotates the plane of polarization by a certain angle. Let the angle of rotation is θ . After passing through the solution, the light is still plane-polarized, but its axis has shifted by θ . Using Malus's Law when completely plane-polarized light is incident on an analyzer, the intensity I of the light transmitted by the analyzer is directly proportional to the square of the cosine of the angle between the transmission axes of the analyzer and the polarizer. I = I incident cos 2 ⁡ ( φ ) Here, φ is the final angle between the light's polarization plane (rotated by the solution) and the analyzer's axis. The initial intensity after polarizer is I 1 = I 0 2 and the final intensity after analyser is I = 3 8 I 0 . Since the analyzer and polarizer were originally at 0 ∘ , the angle φ in Malus's law is the rotation angle θ caused by the solution. Substituting the values into Malus's Law: 3 8 I 0 = ( I 0 2 ) cos 2 ⁡ θ ⇒ 3 / 8 1 / 2 = cos 2 ⁡ ( θ ) ⇒ 3 4 = cos 2 ⁡ ( θ ) ⇒ cos ⁡ ( θ ) = √ 3 2 ⇒ θ = 30 ∘ Therefore, the angle of rotation is 30 degrees.",
