    "explanationEn": "The magnetic force F ⃗ m acting on a charged particle is given by F ⃗ m = q ( v ⃗ × B ⃗ ) . So, the magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity of the particle ( F → m ⟂ v → ) . Since the force is perpendicular to the displacement at every instant, the work done by the magnetic field is always zero: W m = ∫ F → m ⋅ d r → = 0 The electric force F → e is given by F → e = q E → . The electric field in the region is given as E → = E o e − λ x x ˆ , the force is: F → e = qE o e − λ x x ˆ The work done by the electric field as the particle moves from x = 0 to x = L is : W e = ∫ L 0 F → ← e ⋅ d x → ⇒ W e = ∫ L 0 q E o e − λ x ⋅ d x ⇒ W e = e E 0 ∫ L 0 e − λ x ⋅ d x ⇒ W e = e E 0 [ e − λ x − λ ] L 0 ⇒ W e = − q E o λ [ e − λ L − e 0 ] ⇒ W e = − q E o λ [ e − λ L − 1 ] ⇒ W e = q E o λ [ 1 − e − λ L ] Using work-energy theory, the total work done on particle is equal to total change in kinetic energy. Δ K = W total = W m + W e Δ K = 0 + q E o λ [ 1 − e − λ L ] Δ K = q E o λ [ 1 − e − λ L ] Therefore, the change in kinetic energy is qE 0 λ [ 1 − e − λ L ] . Thus, the correct option is (A).",
