    "date": "Sat, 4 Apr 2026",
    "shift": 2,
    "subject": "Mathematics",
    "questionEn": "Let f be a twice differentiable function such that f ( x ) = ∫ x 0 tan ⁡ ( t − x ) d t − ∫ x 0 f ( t ) tan ⁡ t d t , x ∈ ( − π 2 , π 2 ) Then f ′ ′ ( π 6 ) + 12 f ′ ( − π 6 ) + f ( π 6 ) is equal to _______.",
    "optionsEn": [
      "Value 1",
      "Value 2",
      "Value 3",
      "Value 4"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Let f ( x ) = ∫ x 0 tan ⁡ ( t − x ) d t − ∫ x 0 f ( t ) tan ⁡ t d t , x ∈ ( − π 2 , π 2 ) . We have to find f ′′ ( π 6 ) + 12 f ′ ( − π 6 ) + f ( π 6 ) . Step 1: Simplify the first integral Consider ∫ x 0 tan ⁡ ( t − x ) d t . Put u = t − x ⟹ d u = d t . When t = 0 , u = − x , and when t = x , u = 0 . So, ∫ x 0 tan ⁡ ( t − x ) d t = ∫ 0 − x tan ⁡ u d u . Now, ∫ tan ⁡ u d u = − ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ u ) . Hence, ∫ 0 − x tan ⁡ u d u = [ − ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ u ) ] 0 − x = − ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ 0 ) + ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ ( − x ) ) . Since cos ⁡ 0 = 1 and cos ⁡ ( − x ) = cos ⁡ x , ∫ x 0 tan ⁡ ( t − x ) d t = ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ x ) . Therefore the given equation becomes f ( x ) = ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ x ) − ∫ x 0 f ( t ) tan ⁡ t d t . So, f ( x ) + ∫ x 0 f ( t ) tan ⁡ t d t = ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ x ) . Step 2: Differentiate the equation Differentiate both sides with respect to x . Using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, d d x ( ∫ x 0 f ( t ) tan ⁡ t d t ) = f ( x ) tan ⁡ x . Also, d d x ( ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ x ) ) = − tan ⁡ x . Thus, f ′ ( x ) + f ( x ) tan ⁡ x = − tan ⁡ x . So, f ′ ( x ) = − ( f ( x ) + 1 ) tan ⁡ x . Step 3: Find f ( x ) explicitly Now observe that f ( x ) = − 1 satisfies the differential equation: f ′ ( x ) = 0 , − ( f ( x ) + 1 ) tan ⁡ x = 0. Also from the original equation, put x = 0 : f ( 0 ) = ∫ 0 0 tan ⁡ ( t − 0 ) d t − ∫ 0 0 f ( t ) tan ⁡ t d t = 0. So, f ( 0 ) = 0. Let us solve the differential equation properly. From f ′ ( x ) + ( f ( x ) + 1 ) tan ⁡ x = 0 , put y ( x ) = f ( x ) + 1. Then y ′ ( x ) + y ( x ) tan ⁡ x = 0. This gives y ′ y = − tan ⁡ x . Integrating, ln ⁡ | y | = ∫ − tan ⁡ x d x = ln ⁡ ( cos ⁡ x ) + C . Hence, y = C cos ⁡ x . Therefore, f ( x ) + 1 = C cos ⁡ x . Using f ( 0 ) = 0 , 0 + 1 = C cos ⁡ 0 = C . So C = 1 , and hence f ( x ) + 1 = cos ⁡ x which gives f ( x ) = cos ⁡ x − 1. Step 4: Find derivatives Since f ( x ) = cos ⁡ x − 1 , we get f ′ ( x ) = − sin ⁡ x , and f ′′ ( x ) = − cos ⁡ x . Now compute required values: 1. f ′′ ( π 6 ) f ′′ ( π 6 ) = − cos ⁡ ( π 6 ) = − √ 3 2 . 2. f ′ ( − π 6 ) f ′ ( − π 6 ) = − sin ⁡ ( − π 6 ) = 1 2 . So, 12 f ′ ( − π 6 ) = 12 ⋅ 1 2 = 6. 3. f ( π 6 ) f ( π 6 ) = cos ⁡ ( π 6 ) − 1 = √ 3 2 − 1. Step 5: Add them f ′′ ( π 6 ) + 12 f ′ ( − π 6 ) + f ( π 6 ) = − √ 3 2 + 6 + ( √ 3 2 − 1 ) = 6 − 1 = 5. Hence, the required value is 5 .",
