    "explanationEn": "Let the node between C 1 and C 2 be X , The wire (short circuit) connects terminal A to the node between C 2 and C 3 . Thus, this node is at the same potential as A. So, capacitor C 1 is connected between A and X . The capacitor C 2 is connected between A and X . The capacitor C 3 is connected between A and B . The capacitor C 4 is connected between X and B . So, the simplified circuit is, Given values of capacitances are, C 1 = 1 μ F , C 2 = 1 μ F , C 3 = 1 μ F and C 4 = 2 μ F Both C 1 and C 2 are connected between point A and point X in parallel. The equivalent capacitance C AX is : C A X = C 1 + C 2 ⇒ C A X = 1 μ F + 1 μ F = 2 μ F Now, the combination C AX is in series with C 4 because the charge must flow through C AX to reach C 4 . So, the equivalent capacitance ( C ′ ) is 1 C ′ = 1 C AX + 1 C 4 ⇒ 1 C ′ = 1 2 + 1 2 = 1 ⇒ C ′ = 1 μ F The entire top branch ( C ′ ) is now in parallel with C 3 , as both are connected across the main terminals A and B . The total equivalent capacitance C eq is : C eq = C ′ + C 3 ⇒ C eq = 1 μ F + 1 μ F ⇒ C eq = 2 μ F Therefore, the equivalent capacitance between A and B is 2 μ F . Thus, the correct option is (A).",
