    "explanationEn": "For the parabola P : y 2 = 4 k x its latus rectum is the line x = k and the endpoints of the latus rectum are ( k , 2 k ) and ( k , − 2 k ) . So, if the line segment joining the points of intersection of the parabola and ellipse is the latus rectum of the parabola, then the two curves intersect at ( k , ± 2 k ) . Now for the ellipse E : x 2 a 2 + y 2 b 2 = 1 , its latus rectum is the chord through a focus perpendicular to the major axis. Since the common chord is vertical, the major axis of the ellipse must be along the x -axis. Hence the foci are ( ± a e , 0 ) , where e = √ 1 − b 2 a 2 . The latus rectum of the ellipse is the vertical line through a focus: x = a e . Its endpoints are ( a e , ± b 2 a ) . But this same segment is also the latus rectum of the parabola, whose endpoints are ( k , ± 2 k ) . Therefore, comparing coordinates: k = a e and 2 k = b 2 a . Substitute k = a e into the second equation: 2 a e = b 2 a . So, b 2 = 2 a 2 e . Now for the ellipse, b 2 = a 2 ( 1 − e 2 ) . Hence, a 2 ( 1 − e 2 ) = 2 a 2 e . Dividing by a 2 : 1 − e 2 = 2 e . So, e 2 + 2 e − 1 = 0. Since eccentricity is positive, e = − 1 + √ 2 . Now calculate: e 2 + 2 √ 2 . First, e 2 = ( √ 2 − 1 ) 2 = 2 + 1 − 2 √ 2 = 3 − 2 √ 2 . Therefore, e 2 + 2 √ 2 = 3. So the required value is 3 .",
