    "explanationEn": "The Einstein's photoelectric equation states that the energy of incident photons to the work function of the metal and the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons. K max = h ν − Φ 0 Where K max is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons, h ν is the energy of the incident photon (where h is Planck's constant and ν is frequency) and Φ 0 is the work function of the metal, which is the minimum energy required to eject an electron. The work function can also be expressed in terms of the threshold frequency ( v 0 ) , which is the minimum frequency required for photoelectric emission: Φ 0 = h v 0 The stopping potential ( V 0 ) is the negative potential applied to stop the most energetic photoelectrons. The work done by this potential equals the maximum kinetic energy: K max = eV 0 Substituting this into the photoelectric equation: eV 0 = h v − φ ⇒ V 0 = ( h e ) v − Φ 0 e The equation V 0 = ( h e ) v − Φ 0 is in the form of a straight line ( y = mx + c ) . The slope m = h e = constant for all metals, which is why the lines are parallel. X -intercept represents the threshold frequency ( v 0 ) , where V 0 = 0 . At this frequency, the incident energy exactly equals the work function ( Φ 0 = h ν 0 ) . From the graph, we can identify the threshold frequencies for each metal: For X 1 : v 0 ≈ 1.0 × 10 14 Hz For X 2 : v 0 ≈ 1.5 × 10 14 Hz For X 3 : v 0 ≈ 2.0 × 10 14 Hz We can see that v 01 v 02 v 03 . This means the work function of metal X 1 is the lowest ( Φ 1 Φ 2 Φ 3 ) . Now, considering incident radiation of the same wavelength ( λ ). Since energy E = h c λ , the incident energy is the same for all three metals. Substituting this into the energy equation: K max = E incident − Φ 0 Since E incident is constant, the metal with the smallest work function ( Φ 0 ) will result in the greatest kinetic energy ( K max ). As metal X 1 has the lowest threshold frequency and thus the lowest work function, it will emit electrons with the greatest kinetic energy. Hence, the correct option is (A).",
