    "explanationEn": "For a positive borax bead test , the ion must generally have unpaired electrons in its d -orbitals, because transition metal ions give characteristic bead colours due to electronic transitions. Let us check each ion: F e 2 + : [ A r ] 3 d 6 → has unpaired electrons, so it gives borax bead test. F e 3 + : [ A r ] 3 d 5 → has unpaired electrons, so it gives borax bead test. C r 2 + : [ A r ] 3 d 4 → has unpaired electrons, so it gives borax bead test. Z n 2 + : [ A r ] 3 d 10 → all electrons are paired, so it does not give a positive borax bead test. So, among the given ions, we compare F e 2 + , F e 3 + , C r 2 + for highest ionisation enthalpy . Now, higher positive charge and greater effective nuclear charge generally mean higher ionisation enthalpy. Also, F e 3 + has the stable half-filled configuration: F e 3 + = [ A r ] 3 d 5 This makes removal of another electron more difficult. Therefore, the ion that: shows positive borax bead test , and has highest ionisation enthalpy is F e 3 + So, the correct option is D. F e 3 + .",
