    "explanationEn": "Let the given line be written in parametric form by taking x − 1 2 = y − 2 1 = z − α 3 = r . Then a general point A on the line is A ( 1 + 2 r , 2 + r , α + 3 r ) . Given P ( a , b , 0 ) and the midpoint of P A is ( 0 , 3 4 , − 1 4 ) . Using midpoint formula coordinate-wise: 2 r + 1 + a 2 = 0 ⇒ 2 r + a = − 1 r + 2 + b 2 = 3 4 ⇒ r + b = − 1 2 Similarly, from the z -coordinate of the midpoint: 3 r + α 2 = − 1 4 ⇒ 3 r + α = − 1 2 Now, A is the foot of the perpendicular from P to the line, so P A → ← is perpendicular to the direction vector of the line. The direction ratios of the line are ( 2 , 1 , 3 ) , so we use the dot product condition: 2 ( x A − a ) + 1 ( y A − b ) + 3 ( z A − 0 ) = 0. Using the midpoint point ( 0 , 3 4 , − 1 4 ) , we can write P A → ← = 2 ( ( 0 , 3 4 , − 1 4 ) − P ) , so its components are proportional to ( − a , 3 4 − b , − 1 4 ) . Hence the perpendicularity condition becomes: 2 ⋅ a + 1 ⋅ ( b − 3 / 4 ) + 3 ⋅ 1 4 = 0 ⇒ a + b = 0 From 2 r + a = − 1 , we get a = − 1 − 2 r . From r + b = − 1 2 , we get b = − 1 2 − r . Substitute in a + b = 0 : ⇒ 2 ( − 1 − 2 r ) + ( − r − 1 / 2 ) = 0 ⇒ − 3 / 2 = 3 r = 1 ⇒ r = − 1 / 2 , a = 0 , b = 0 , α = 1 ∴ a 2 + b 2 + α 2 = 1",
