    "subject": "Mathematics",
    "questionEn": "Let f be a differentiable function satisfying f ( x ) = 1 − 2 x + ∫ 0 x e ( x − t ) f ( t ) d t , x ∈ R and let g ( x ) = ∫ 0 x ( f ( t ) + 2 ) 15 ( t − 4 ) 6 ( t + 12 ) 17 d t , x ∈ R . If p and q are respectively the points of local minima and local maxima of g , then the value of | p + q | is equal to ________.",
    "optionsEn": [
      "Value 1",
      "Value 2",
      "Value 3",
      "Value 4"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "The given equation is: f ( x ) = 1 − 2 x + ∫ x 0 e ( x − t ) f ( t ) d t Since e ( x − t ) = e x ⋅ e − t , we can move e x outside the integral because it is constant relative to t f ( x ) = 1 − 2 x + e x ∫ x 0 e − t f ( t ) d t Differentiate both sides with respect to x using the Leibniz Rule and the product rule: f ′ ( x ) = − 2 + [ e x ∫ x 0 e − t f ( t ) d t + e x ( e − x f ( x ) ) ] Now, we use substitution from our original equation. Notice that e x ∫ x 0 e − t f ( t ) d t = f ( x ) − ( 1 − 2 x ) . Substituting this back into the derivative expression: f ′ ( x ) = − 2 + [ f ( x ) − 1 + 2 x ] + f ( x ) ⇒ f ′ ( x ) = 2 f ( x ) + 2 x − 3 Combine the terms to get the linear differential equation in the form of d f ( x ) d x + P ( x ) f ( x ) = Q ( x ) d f ( x ) d x − 2 f ( x ) = 2 x − 3 Solve for f ( x ) Integrating Factor (IF): e ∫ − 2 d x = e − 2 x General Solution: f ( x ) ⋅ e − 2 x = ∫ ( 2 x − 3 ) e − 2 x d x By integrating the right side (Integration by Parts), we get f ( x ) = 1 − x + C e 2 x . Using f ( 0 ) = 1 (from the original equation), we find C = 0 . Result: f ( x ) = 1 − x . Find the Extrema of g ( x ) g ( x ) = ∫ x 0 ( f ( t ) + 2 ) 15 ( t − 4 ) 6 ( t + 12 ) 17 d t Differentiate both sides with respect to x using the Leibniz Rule g ′ ( x ) = ( f ( x ) + 2 ) 15 ( x − 4 ) 6 ( x + 12 ) 17 Substitute f ( x ) = 1 − x : g ′ ( x ) = ( 1 − x + 2 ) 15 ( x − 4 ) 6 ( x + 12 ) 17 g ′ ( x ) = ( 3 − x ) 15 ( x − 4 ) 6 ( x + 12 ) 17 Critical points: x = 3 , 4 , − 12 . Plot on number line to find local maxima and minima. Local Minima ( p ): At x = − 12 , g ′ ( x ) changes sign from negative to positive. Thus, p = − 12 . Local Maxima ( q ): At x = 3 , g ′ ( x ) changes sign from positive to negative. Thus, q = 3 . x = 4 : No sign change occurs (even power 6), so it is not a local extremum. Final Answer | p + q | = | − 12 + 3 | = | − 9 | = 9 The value of | p + q | is 9 .",
