    "explanationEn": "For an ideal solution, by Raoult’s law: P total = x A P 0 A + x B P 0 B where P 0 A , P 0 B are vapour pressures of pure liquids A and B. Step 1: First mixture (2 mol A + 3 mol B) Total moles = 2 + 3 = 5 x A = 2 5 , x B = 3 5 Given P total = 320 mm Hg: 320 = 2 5 P 0 A + 3 5 P 0 B Multiply by 5: 1600 = 2 P 0 A + 3 P 0 B . . . ( 1 ) Step 2: After adding 1 mol A and 1 mol B New moles: A = 3 , B = 4 , total = 7 x A = 3 7 , x B = 4 7 Given new vapour pressure = 328.6 mm Hg: 328.6 = 3 7 P 0 A + 4 7 P 0 B Multiply by 7: 2300.2 = 3 P 0 A + 4 P 0 B . . . ( 2 ) Step 3: Solve the two equations From (1): 2 P 0 A + 3 P 0 B = 1600 From (2): 3 P 0 A + 4 P 0 B = 2300.2 Multiply (1) by 3: 6 P 0 A + 9 P 0 B = 4800 Multiply (2) by 2: 6 P 0 A + 8 P 0 B = 4600.4 Subtract: P 0 B = 4800 − 4600.4 = 199.6 ≈ 200 Put in (1): 2 P 0 A + 3 ( 199.6 ) = 1600 2 P 0 A + 598.8 = 1600 2 P 0 A = 1001.2 ⇒ P 0 A = 500.6 ≈ 500 Final Answer Vapour pressures of A and B are approximately: P 0 A = 500 mm Hg , P 0 B = 200 mm Hg Correct option: C (500, 200)",
