    "explanationEn": "For hydrogen, the energy difference for a spectral line is given by Δ E = 13.6 × Z 2 ( 1 n 2 1 − 1 n 2 2 ) Step 1: Identify the transitions In the Lyman series, electrons fall to n 1 = 1 . The first line L 1 is the transition 2 → 1 . In the Balmer series, electrons fall to n 1 = 2 . The first line B 1 is the transition 3 → 2 . Step 2: Find energy of L 1 Δ E ( L 1 ) = 13.6 × Z 2 ( 1 1 2 − 1 2 2 ) = 13.6 Z 2 × 3 4 Step 3: Find energy of B 1 Δ E ( B 1 ) = 13.6 × Z 2 × ( 1 2 2 − 1 3 2 ) = 13.6 × Z 2 × 5 4 × 9 Step 4: Take the ratio to get x The factor 13.6 Z 2 cancels in the ratio. Δ E ( L 1 ) Δ E ( B 1 ) = 3 5 × 9 = 27 5 = x Step 5: Write in the asked form x = ( 27 5 × 10 ) × 10 − 1 = 54 × 10 − 1",
