    "explanationEn": "A meter bridge works on the principle of a Wheatstone bridge. When the bridge is balanced (null point), no current flows through the galvanometer. The relationship between the resistors in the gaps ( R 1 , R 2 ) and the balancing lengths ( l , 100 − l ) is: R 1 R 2 = l 100 − l Here, R 1 = 2 Ω , R 2 = 3 Ω Length of the wire from the left end ( X ) to the null point is l 1 then remaining length of the wire (assuming standard 100 cm wire) is ( 100 − l 1 ) . 2 3 = l 1 100 − l 1 ⇒ 2 ( 100 − l 1 ) = 3 l 1 ⇒ 200 − 2 l 1 = 3 l 1 ⇒ 200 = 5 l 1 ⇒ l 1 = 40 cm So, the initial null point is at 40 cm from X. When an unknown resistor X is connected in parallel with the 3 Ω resistor in the right gap. The new right gap resistance is R ′ 2 . For two resistors in parallel, the equivalent resistance is: R ′ 2 = 3 × X 3 + X The null point shifts by 22.5 cm towards Y (the right end). l 2 = l 1 + 22.5 cm ⇒ l 2 = 40 + 22.5 = 62.5 cm Using balanced condition, R 1 R ′ 2 = l 2 100 − l 2 ⇒ 2 R ′ 2 = 62.5 100 − 62.5 ⇒ 2 R ′ 2 = 62.5 37.5 = 5 3 R ′ 2 = 6 5 = 1.2 Ω We know that R ′ 2 is the parallel combination of 3 Ω and X . 1.2 = 3 X 3 + X ⇒ 1.2 ( 3 + X ) = 3 X ⇒ 3.6 + 1.2 X = 3 X ⇒ 3.6 = 3 X − 1.2 X ⇒ 3.6 = 1.8 X ⇒ X = 2 Ω Therefore, the value of X is 2 Ω . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
