    "explanationEn": "The heavier mass starts from rest ( u = 0 ) and falls a certain distance ( h ) in time ( t ). Using the kinematic equation : h = ut + 1 2 at 2 ⇒ h = 1 2 at 2 ⇒ a = 2 h t 2 The given distance h = 81 cm = 0.81 m The time taken is t = 9 s a = 2 × 0.81 9 2 = 1.62 81 ⇒ a = 0.02 m / s 2 Since the pulley has mass (and inertia), the tension is not the same on both sides. Let T 1 be the tension on the heavier mass side ( m 1 ) and T 2 be the tension on the lighter mass side ( m 2 ). For heavier mass ( m 1 = 0.4 kg ) which is moving down. m 1 g − T 1 = m 1 a ⇒ T 1 = m 1 ( g − a ) ... ( i ) For lighter mass ( m 2 = 0.35 kg ) which is moving up. T 2 − m 2 g = m 2 a ⇒ T 2 = m 2 ( g + a ) ... (ii) The pulley rotates due to the difference in tensions. The net torque ( τ ) causes angular acceleration ( α ) . τ = ( T 1 − T 2 ) R Also, from Newton's Second Law for rotation: τ = I α Therefore : ( T 1 − T 2 ) R = I α Assuming the string does not slip, the linear acceleration of the string a = R α ⇒ α = a R Substituting in the torque equation : ( T 1 − T 2 ) R = I ( a R ) I = ( T 1 − T 2 ) R 2 a ... ( i i i ) On subtracting (ii) from (i) T 1 − T 2 = [ m 1 ( g − a ) ] − [ m 2 ( g + a ) ] ⇒ T 1 − T 2 = ( m 1 g − m 1 a ) − ( m 2 g + m 2 a ) ⇒ T 1 − T 2 = ( m 1 − m 2 ) g − ( m 1 + m 2 ) a Putting the values, T 1 − T 2 = ( 0.400 − 0.350 ) × 9.8 − ( 0.400 + 0.350 ) × 0.02 ⇒ T 1 − T 2 = ( 0.05 × 9.8 ) − ( 0.75 × 0.02 ) ⇒ T 1 − T 2 = 0.475 N So, putting in equation (iii), I = ( 0.475 ) × R 2 a Given radius R = 2 cm = 0.02 m . I = 0.475 × ( 0.02 ) 2 0.02 = 0.475 × 0.02 = 9.5 × 10 − 3 kgm 2 Therefore, the rotational inertia of the pulley is 9.5 × 10 − 3 kgm 2 . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
