    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "For relation A The equation ∮ E ⃗ ⋅ d l ⃗ = − d d t [ ∮ B ⃗ ⋅ d a ⃗ ] mathematically states that the line integral of the electric field around a closed loop is equal to the negative rate of change of magnetic flux through the loop. This is the integral form of Faraday's Law of Electromagnetic Induction, i.e., A → II For relation B This equation ∮ B → ⋅ d l → = μ 0 ( I + ε 0 d Φ E dt ) relates the magnetic field line integral to both the conduction current (I) and the displacement current ( ε 0 d Φ E dt ) . This modification to Ampere's Law was introduced by Maxwell to account for changing electric fields. This is the Ampere-Maxwell Law, i.e., B → III For relation C In relation C , ∮ E ⃗ ⋅ d a ⃗ = 1 ε 0 ∫ V ρ d V the total charge Q enclosed is represented as ∮ E ⃗ ⋅ d a ⃗ . The equation states that the total electric flux through a closed surface is equal to the enclosed charge divided by ε 0 . This is Gauss's Law of Electrostatics, i.e., C → IV For relation D This relation ∮ B → ⋅ d l → = μ 0 I states that the line integral of the magnetic field around a closed loop is equal to μ 0 times the current enclosed by the loop. This is the original form of Ampere's Circuital Law which is valid for steady currents, i.e., D → I So, the correct match is A → II, B → III, C → IV, D → I. Hence, the correct option is (B).",
