    "explanationEn": "When a straight wire falls through a magnetic field, an EMF gets induced across its ends. This is the motional EMF, and it is given by ε = B v l where B is the magnetic field, v is the speed of the wire, and l is its length. Here the wire is falling freely, so first we need its speed after falling through 200 m. Using the equation of motion with initial velocity zero, v 2 = 2 g h = 2 × 10 × 200 = 4000 v = √ 4000 = 20 √ 10 m/s Now, the magnetic field is given in Gauss, so we convert it to SI units. Since 1 Gauss = 10 − 4 T, B = 0.5 Gauss = 0.5 × 10 − 4 T The wire is horizontal, the field is horizontal and perpendicular to the wire, and the motion is vertical. So B , v and l are all mutually perpendicular, and we can directly use ε = B v l . ε = 0.5 × 10 − 4 × 20 √ 10 × 20 ε = 0.5 × 10 − 4 × 400 √ 10 = 200 √ 10 × 10 − 4 V ε = 0.02 √ 10 V = 20 √ 10 mV So the induced EMF across the wire is 20 √ 10 mV, which is Option C.",
