    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "For a satellite revolving in a circular orbit of radius r around the Earth (mass M ), the time period ( T ) is given by Kepler's Third Law For the equilibrium along the radial direction, F g = m v 2 r ⇒ G M e m r 2 = m v 2 r ⇒ v = √ G M e r So, the time period of the revolution is, T = 2 π r v = 2 π r √ GM e r ⇒ T = 2 π √ r 3 GM e where G is the universal gravitational constant and M e is the mass of Earth. If the satellite is orbiting very close to the Earth's surface, its orbital radius r is approximately equal to the Earth's radius, R e . T ≈ 2 π √ R 3 e GM e Assuming the Earth is a uniform sphere, its mass M is the product of its volume and density: M = 4 3 π R 3 e ρ Substitute this mass into our time period equation : T = 2 π √ R 3 e G ( 4 3 π R 3 e ρ ) = 2 π √ 3 4 π G ρ ⇒ T = √ 3 π G ρ Since 3 , π , and G are all constants, this equation proves that the time period of a satellite orbiting very close to the Earth's surface depends entirely on the density ( ρ ) of the Earth. Therefore, Statement I is true. We also know the formula for the acceleration due to gravity (g) on the surface of the Earth : g = GM e R 2 e ⇒ GM = gR 2 e Substitute this expression for GM e into the time period equation T ≈ 2 π √ R 3 e GM e T = 2 π √ R 3 e gR 2 e = 2 π √ R e g Therefore, Statement II is also true. As both statement I and statement II are true, so the correct option is (C).",
