    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Let a conservative force F → is acting on an object. For infinitesimally small displacement d r → = dx i ˆ + dy j ˆ + dz k ˆ , the work done is, dW = F → ⋅ d r → The work done (W) by a variable force F ⃗ when displacing an object from an initial position vector r ⃗ 1 to a final position vector r → 2 is the total work done in moving the object, The work done is, W = ∫ r 1 r 2 F ⃗ ⋅ d r ⃗ So, this formula is valid for all forces whether it is conservative or non-conservative force. But the statement I states that the work done is W = − ∫ r 2 r 1 F ⃗ . d r ⃗ . Hence, the statement I is false. Work done by a conservative force is path independent. And the work done by the conservative force is negative of the change in potential energy, i.e., the work done by the conservative force is stored in object as potential energy. The work done depends only on the initial and final positions. W = ∫ r → 1 r → 2 F → ← ⋅ d r → = − ( U f − U i ) For a particle going from point A to point B , there are infinitely many possible paths. So, it is a geometrical fact and is correct. A force is called conservative if ∮ F → ⋅ d r → = 0 That means work done in a closed path is zero. Hence for motion from A to B : W A → B = U A − U B So work done depends only on initial position and final position and not on the path followed. But the statement says, \"work done changes with path for a conservative force\", so the statement is incorrect because for conservative forces, straight path, curved path, zig-zag path all give the same work between the same two points. Hence, the statement II is incorrect. Therefore, both Statement I and Statement II are false. Hence, the correct option is (C).",
