    "explanationEn": "Looking at the coils along their common axis (from the right side looking left). Coil C 1 carries current in the anti-clockwise ( ACW ) direction. According to the Right-Hand Grip Rule, an ACW current produces a magnetic field ( B → 1 ) pointing towards the observer. Let's define this direction towards the observer as positive flux. Coil C 3 : Carries current in the clockwise (CW) direction. A CW current produces a magnetic field ( B → 3 ) pointing away from the observer. Let's define this direction pointing away from the observer as negative flux. Coil C 2 is exactly in the middle. Because C 1 and C 3 are identical and carry the same magnitude of current I , the strength of their magnetic fields at the midpoint will be identical. Since B ⃗ 1 points towards us and B ⃗ 3 points away from us with equal magnitudes, they cancel each other out. Initial Net Magnetic Flux through C 2 is Φ initial = 0 A clockwise current in C 2 would create its own induced magnetic field ( B → induced ) pointing away from the observer i.e., negative direction. Lenz's Law states that an induced current will always flow in a direction that opposes the change in magnetic flux that caused it. Since the induced field points in the negative direction, it must be resisting a change in the positive direction. Therefore, the external magnetic flux through C 2 must be increasing in the positive direction (towards the observer). To get a net increase in positive flux, we have two ways : Increase the positive flux from C 1 by moving the source of the positive flux ( C 1 ) closer to C 2 . Decrease the negative flux from C 3 by moving the source of the negative flux ( C 3 ) further away from C 2 . Therefore, C 1 moves towards C 2 (increases positive flux) and C 3 moves away from C 2 (decreases negative flux). This results in a net positive flux change required to induce a clockwise current. Hence, the correct option is (B).",
