    "explanationEn": "Einstein's equation tells us that mass and energy are interchangeable. E = mc 2 When a nuclear reaction occurs, the total mass of the products is often slightly different from the initial mass of the reactants. If the products are lighter, the missing mass is released as energy. If the products are heavier, energy must be supplied to create that extra mass. It is given that a particle breaks into 4 α particles. Initial mass of the single particle. M initial = 15.348 amu Final mass of 4 α particles = M final = 4 × mass of He nucleus M final = 4 × 4.002 amu = 16.008 amu The mass defect ( Δ m ) during this nuclear reaction is, Δ m = M final − M initial ⇒ Δ m = 16.008 amu − 15.348 amu ⇒ Δ m = 0.66 amu It is given that 1 amu = 1.66 × 10 − 27 kg . Δ m = 0.66 × ( 1.66 × 10 − 27 ) kg ⇒ Δ m = 1.0956 × 10 − 27 kg Now using Einstein's equation, E = Δ m ⋅ c 2 ⇒ E = ( 1.0956 × 10 − 27 ) × ( 3 × 10 8 ) 2 ⇒ E = ( 1.0956 × 10 − 27 ) × ( 9 × 10 16 ) ⇒ E = 9.8604 × 10 − 11 Joules This energy is provided by a single photon. The energy of a photon is directly proportional to its frequency ( v ), given by Planck's equation: E = h ν Where h is Planck's constant ( 6.6 × 10 − 34 J ⋅ s ) . v = E h ⇒ v = 9.8604 × 10 − 11 6.6 × 10 − 34 ⇒ v = 1.494 × 10 23 Hz ⇒ v kHz = 1.494 × 10 23 × 10 − 3 kHz ⇒ v kHz = 14.94 × 10 19 kHz Therefore, the minimum frequency of photon required is 14.94 × 10 19 kHz . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
