    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Let an α -particle fired directly at the center of a target nucleus. The α -particle has maximum kinetic energy ( K ) and practically zero electrostatic potential energy at very far away from target material. As it gets closer, the positive charge of the α -particle and the positive charge of the target nucleus repel each other. The α -particle slows down, converting its kinetic energy into electrostatic potential energy. At the closest approach ( r 0 ) it momentarily stops before reversing direction. At this exact point, all its initial kinetic energy has been converted into potential energy. By the Law of Conservation of Energy: Initial Kinetic Energy ( K 0 ) = Final Potential Energy ( U ) The electrostatic potential energy between two point charges ( q 1 and q 2 ) separated by a distance r is : U = 1 4 π ε 0 q 1 q 2 r An α -particle is a Helium nucleus, so it has 2 protons. So, the charge of α -particle ( q 1 ) is q 1 = + 2 e The atomic number of the target is Z = 79 , so it has 79 protons, So, the charge of target nucleus ( q 2 ) is q 2 = + 79 e Substituting into our energy conservation equation : K 0 = 1 4 π ε 0 ( 2 e ) ( 79 e ) r 0 ⇒ r 0 = 1 4 π ε 0 158 e 2 K 0 The kinetic energy of alpha particle is, K 0 = 7.9 MeV = 7.9 × 10 6 eV Since 1 eV = 1.6 × 10 − 19 J , we get: K 0 = ( 7.9 × 10 6 ) × ( 1.6 × 10 − 19 ) J ⇒ K 0 = 12.64 × 10 − 13 J Putting in the closest approach formula, r 0 = ( 9 × 10 9 ) × 158 × ( 1.6 × 10 − 19 ) 2 12.64 × 10 − 13 ⇒ r 0 = ( 9 × 158 × 2.56 ) × 10 − 29 12.64 × 10 − 13 ⇒ r 0 = 288 × 10 − 16 m ⇒ r 0 = 2.88 × 10 − 14 m Therefore, the distance of closest approach is r 0 = 2.88 × 10 − 14 m . The distance from the centre of the nucleus to this stopping point is the distance of closest approach. Because the α -particle cannot physically penetrate the nucleus using just the electrostatic force in this classical model, r 0 gives us an upper estimate for the radius of the target nucleus. Diameter = 2 × Radius Diameter = 2 × r 0 Diameter = 2 × ( 2.88 × 10 − 14 m ) Diameter = 5.76 × 10 − 14 m Therefore, the diameter of nuclei of the target material is approximately 5.76 × 10 − 14 m . Hence, the correct option is (B).",
