    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "When an inductor is connected to a DC voltage source (V) with a resistance (R), it initially opposes the flow of current. Over time, the current increases until it reaches a steady, maximum state. Once the current is steady, the inductor acts like an ideal wire (zero resistance). Therefore, the maximum current ( i max ) is determined by Ohm's Law : i max = V R For given circuit : Voltage V = 10 V Resistance R = 10 Ω i max = 10 10 = 1 A The current in the circuit is 1 e time the maximum value, i = i max × ( 1 e ) i = 1 × ( 1 e ) = 1 e A For a long solenoid, the energy stored in an inductor is: U = 1 2 Li 2 The inductance of a long solenoid is: L = μ 0 N 2 A l , where N is total turns, A is cross-sectional area, and l is length. The volume of the cylindrical core of the solenoid is: V vol = A × 1 Then the energy density formula is u = energy ⁡ ( U ) volume ⁡ ( V vol ) u = 1 2 Li 2 A ⋅ l ⇒ u = 1 2 ( μ 0 N 2 A l ) i 2 A ⋅ l ⇒ u = μ 0 N 2 i 2 2 l 2 ⇒ u = 1 2 μ 0 n 2 i 2 Where n = N l is the number of turns per unit length. Putting the given values into the derived energy density formula : μ 0 = 4 π × 10 − 7 Tm / A , n = 10 4 m − 1 , i = 1 e A ; ⇒ u = 1 2 × ( 4 π × 10 − 7 ) × ( 10 4 ) 2 × ( 1 e ) 2 ⇒ u = 20 π × 1 / e 2 J / m 3 ⇒ 20 π × 1 e 2 = α π × 1 e 2 ⇒ α = 20 . Therefore, the value of α is 20.",
