    "explanationEn": "The first reaction is the well-known Chromyl chloride test . When a chloride salt like sodium chloride ( NaCl ) is heated with potassium dichromate ( K 2 Cr 2 O 7 ) and concentrated sulfuric acid ( H 2 SO 4 ), deep red vapors of chromyl chloride are evolved. 4 NaCl + K 2 Cr 2 O 7 + 6 H 2 SO 4 → 2 CrO 2 Cl 2 + 2 KHSO 4 + 4 NaHSO 4 + 3 H 2 O Thus, Product A is Chromyl chloride ( CrO 2 Cl 2 ) . When chromyl chloride gas is passed through an aqueous solution of sodium hydroxide ( NaOH ), a yellow solution of sodium chromate is formed. CrO 2 Cl 2 + 4 NaOH → Na 2 CrO 4 + 2 NaCl + 2 H 2 O Thus, Product B is Sodium chromate ( Na 2 CrO 4 ) . When a solution of a chromate acts with hydrogen peroxide ( H 2 O 2 ) in an acidic medium (provided by H 2 SO 4 ), a deep blue-colored compound known as chromium pentoxide is formed. Na 2 CrO 4 + H 2 SO 4 + 2 H 2 O 2 → CrO 5 + Na 2 SO 4 + 3 H 2 O Thus, Product C is Chromium pentoxide ( CrO 5 ) . Chromium pentoxide has a famous \"butterfly structure\". Finding X : In its structure, there are two peroxide ( O − O ) linkages. Each peroxide linkage represents one O 2 − 2 unit. Therefore, the number of O 2 − 2 units is 2 . So, X = 2 . Finding Y : The chemical formula is CrO 5 , meaning there are 5 oxygen atoms in total. So, Y = 5 . Finding Z : Out of the 5 oxygen atoms in the butterfly structure, one is bonded by a double bond (normal oxide with an oxidation state of − 2 ), and the remaining four form the peroxide linkages (with an oxidation state of − 1 each). Let the oxidation state of Chromium ( Cr ) be Z . We can calculate it as follows: Z + 1 ( − 2 ) + 4 ( − 1 ) = 0 Z − 2 − 4 = 0 Z = + 6 So, Z = 6 . ∴ X + Y + Z = 2 + 5 + 6 = 13",
