    "explanationEn": "Let an α -particle fired directly at the centre of a target nucleus, gold foil. The α -particle has maximum kinetic energy ( K ) and practically zero electrostatic potential energy at very far away from target material. The initial Kinetic Energy ( K 0 ) of the alpha particle is : K 0 = 7.7 MeV As it gets closer, the positive charge of the α -particle and the positive charge of the target nucleus repel each other. The α -particle slows down, converting its kinetic energy into electrostatic potential energy. At the closest approach ( r 0 ) it momentarily stops before reversing direction. At this exact point, all its initial kinetic energy has been converted into potential energy. The electrostatic potential energy ( U ) between two point charges q 1 and q 2 separated by a distance r 0 is : U = 1 4 π ε 0 q 1 q 2 r 0 By the Law of Conservation of Energy : Initial Kinetic Energy ( K 0 ) = Final Potential Energy ( U ) The electrostatic potential energy between two point charges ( q 1 and q 2 ) separated by a distance r is : U = 1 4 π ε 0 q 1 q 2 r An α -particle is a Helium nucleus, so it has 2 protons. So, the charge of α -particle ( q 1 ) is q 1 = + 2 e The atomic number of the target is Z = 79 , so it has 79 protons, So, the charge of target nucleus ( q 2 ) is q 2 = + 79 e Substituting into our energy conservation equation : K 0 = 1 4 π ε 0 ( 2 e ) ( 79 e ) r 0 ⇒ r 0 = 1 4 π ε 0 158 e 2 K 0 The kinetic energy of alpha particle is, K 0 = 7.7 MeV = 7.7 × 10 6 eV Since 1 eV = 1.6 × 10 − 19 J , we get : K 0 = ( 7.7 × 10 6 ) × ( 1.6 × 10 − 19 ) J ⇒ K 0 = 12.32 × 10 − 13 J Putting in the closest approach formula, r 0 = ( 9 × 10 9 ) × 158 × ( 1.6 × 10 − 19 ) 2 12.32 × 10 − 13 ⇒ r 0 = ( 9 × 158 × 2.56 ) × 10 − 29 12.32 × 10 − 13 ⇒ r 0 = 295 × 10 − 16 m ⇒ r 0 = 2.95 × 10 − 14 m Therefore, the distance of closest approach is r 0 = 2.95 × 10 − 14 m . Hence, the correct option is (C).",
