    "explanationEn": "A current-carrying solenoid produces a magnetic field ( B → ). For an ideal solenoid, the magnetic field inside is uniform and directed parallel to its axis. The magnetic force ( F ⃗ m ) on a moving charge Q with velocity v ⃗ is given by the Lorentz force formula : F → m = Q ( v → × B → ) In this case, the charged particle is released from rest along the axis. The magnetic field B ⃗ is also along the axis. Therefore, the velocity v ⃗ (once the particle starts moving) and the magnetic field B ⃗ are parallel to each other. The magnitude of the magnetic force is the cross product of Q ( v → × B → ) , F m = Q v B sin ⁡ θ Since the angle θ between the velocity and the magnetic field is 0 ∘ : F m = Q v B sin ⁡ 0 ∘ The magnetic field exerts no force on the particle moving along the axis. The solenoid is placed vertically. When the particle of mass m is released, the only non-zero force acting on it is the force of gravity ( F → g ) : F → g = mg This force acts vertically downwards along the axis of the solenoid. According to Newton's Second Law ( F net = ma ) : mg = ma ⇒ a = g Since the magnetic force is zero the particle falls freely under gravity. Thus, its acceleration is exactly equal to g. Hence, the correct option is (D).",
