    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "questionEn": "Based on Heisenberg's uncertainty principle, the uncertainty in the velocity of the electron to be found within an atomic nucleus of diameter 10 − 15 m is ________ × 10 9 ms − 1 (nearest integer) [Given : mass of electron = 9.1 × 10 − 31 kg , Planck's constant ( h ) = 6.626 × 10 − 34 Js ] (Value of π = 3.14 )",
    "optionsEn": [
      "Value 1",
      "Value 2",
      "Value 3",
      "Value 4"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "To find the uncertainty in the velocity of the electron within an atomic nucleus of diameter 10 − 15 m , we can use Heisenberg's uncertainty principle. The principle is expressed as: Δ x ⋅ m Δ v ≥ h 4 π Here, Δ x is the uncertainty in position, m is the mass of the electron, Δ v is the uncertainty in velocity, and h is Planck's constant. Given: Δ x = 10 − 15 m m = 9.1 × 10 − 31 kg h = 6.626 × 10 − 34 Js π = 3.14 We need to find Δ v . Rearrange the uncertainty principle to solve for Δ v : Δ v ≈ h 4 π m Δ x Plug in the numbers: Δ v = 6.626 × 10 − 34 4 × 3.14 × 9.1 × 10 − 31 × 10 − 15 First, calculate the denominator: 4 × 3.14 × 9.1 × 10 − 31 × 10 − 15 = 114.296 × 10 − 46 Now, perform the division: Δ v = 6.626 × 10 − 34 114.296 × 10 − 46 = 6.626 114.296 × 10 12 Perform the division: Δ v ≈ 0.0579 × 10 12 m/s = 57.97 × 10 9 m/s So, the uncertainty in the velocity of the electron is: 58 × 10 9 m/s (nearest integer)",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": [
      "Chemistry",
      "JEE Main 2024",
      "April Session"
    ]
