    "explanationEn": "We can start solving this problem by first understanding that the resistance of a material changes with temperature, and this change can be quantified using the temperature coefficient of resistance α . The relationship between the resistance of a material at any temperature T and its resistance at a reference temperature T 0 is given by the formula: R = R 0 ( 1 + α ( T − T 0 ) ) Where: R is the resistance at temperature T (in this case, 62 Ω ). R 0 is the resistance at reference temperature T 0 (in this case, 50 Ω ). α is the temperature coefficient of resistance (in this case, 2.4 × 10 − 4 ∘ C − 1 ). T is the unknown temperature we need to find. T 0 is the reference temperature, given as 27 ∘ C . By substituting the given values into the formula, we get: 62 = 50 ( 1 + 2.4 × 10 − 4 ( T − 27 ) ) First, divide both sides of the equation by 50: 62 50 = 1 + 2.4 × 10 − 4 ( T − 27 ) Then solve for T : 1.24 = 1 + 2.4 × 10 − 4 ( T − 27 ) 0.24 = 2.4 × 10 − 4 ( T − 27 ) 0.24 2.4 × 10 − 4 = T − 27 1000 = T − 27 T = 1027 ∘ C Thus, the temperature of the element when its resistance is 62 Ω is 1027 ∘ C .",
