    "explanationEn": "The least count of a vernier caliper is defined as the smallest distance that it can measure and is calculated by the difference in length between one main scale division and one vernier scale division. It can be represented as: Least Count = Main scale division − Vernier scale division Given that one main scale division is equal to m units and the n th division of main scale coincides with the ( n + 1 ) th division of the vernier scale, this means that n divisions on the main scale is equal to ( n + 1 ) divisions on the vernier scale. Since one main scale division is m units, n divisions on the main scale would be n × m units. If n divisions on the main scale are equal to ( n + 1 ) divisions on the vernier scale, we can determine the length of one vernier scale division as Length of one vernier scale division = n × m n + 1 Thus, the least count, which is the difference between one main scale division and one vernier scale division, is: Least Count = m − n × m n + 1 = m ( 1 − n n + 1 ) = m ( n + 1 − n n + 1 ) = m ( 1 n + 1 ) This simplifies to: Least Count = m n + 1 Therefore, the correct option is: Option B: m n + 1",
