    "correctOptionIndex": 1,
    "explanationEn": "For an adiabatic process, the work done by the gas can be found using the formula: W = P 1 V 1 − P 2 V 2 γ − 1 Given that the volume is doubled ( V 2 = 2 V 1 ) and the adiabatic constant γ = 3 2 , we can manipulate the ideal gas law and the adiabatic process relationship to find an expression for work done in terms of the initial conditions and γ . Recall, for an adiabatic process, P V γ = constant , so we can write: P 1 V γ 1 = P 2 V γ 2 Using the fact that V 2 = 2 V 1 , we can express P 2 in terms of P 1 and V 1 as follows: P 1 V γ 1 = P 2 ( 2 V 1 ) γ P 2 = P 1 ( V 1 2 V 1 ) γ P 2 = P 1 ( 1 2 ) γ The work done then becomes: W = P 1 V 1 − P 1 ( 1 2 ) γ ⋅ 2 V 1 γ − 1 W = P 1 V 1 1 − ( 1 2 ) γ ⋅ 2 γ − 1 Plugging in γ = 3 2 , we get: W = P 1 V 1 1 − ( 1 2 ) 3 2 ⋅ 2 3 2 − 1 W = P 1 V 1 1 − √ 1 2 ⋅ 2 1 2 W = 2 P 1 V 1 ( 1 − √ 1 2 ) Since P 1 V 1 = n R T for 1 mole ( n = 1 ) of gas at temperature T , we can further simplify: W = 2 R T ( 1 − √ 1 2 ) W = 2 R T ( 1 − 1 √ 2 ) W = R T ( 2 − √ 2 ) Therefore, the correct option is: Option B RT [ 2 − √ 2 ] .",
