    "explanationEn": "To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of resonance in an LCR (inductor-capacitor-resistor) circuit. At resonance, the inductive reactance and capacitive reactance cancel each other out. The condition for resonance in an LCR circuit is given by: ω L = 1 ω C Where: ω is the angular frequency L is the inductance C is the capacitance Rewriting for angular frequency: ω 2 = 1 L C The angular frequency ω is related to the frequency f by: ω = 2 π f Substituting this into the equation for angular frequency gives: ( 2 π f ) 2 = 1 L C Therefore, the frequency f can be found by: f = 1 2 π √ L C Given values: Inductance, L = 100 mH = 100 × 10 − 3 H Capacitance, C = 2.5 nF = 2.5 × 10 − 9 F Plug these values into the frequency equation: f = 1 2 π √ ( 100 × 10 − 3 ) ( 2.5 × 10 − 9 ) First, calculate the product of L and C : L ⋅ C = 100 × 10 − 3 ⋅ 2.5 × 10 − 9 = 2.5 × 10 − 10 Now, take the square root of the product: √ 2.5 × 10 − 10 = √ 2.5 × 10 − 5 Given that π 2 = 10 , we have: π = √ 10 Since √ 2.5 = √ 10 2 : √ 2.5 × 10 − 5 = √ 10 2 × 10 − 5 Now substitute back into the frequency formula: f = 1 2 π ( √ 10 2 × 10 − 5 ) = 1 π √ 10 × 10 − 5 Simplify the equation: f = 10 5 π √ 10 Given that π ≈ 3.14 , we get: f ≈ 10 5 3.14 × 3.162 Simplify further: f ≈ 10 5 9.93 ≈ 10 × 10 3 Hz Thus, the frequency of the AC source is approximately: 10 × 10 3 Hz",
