    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "questionEn": "Considering acetic acid dissociates in water, its dissociation constant is 6.25 × 10 − 5 . If 5 mL of acetic acid is dissolved in 1 litre water, the solution will freeze at − x × 10 − 2 ∘ C , provided pure water freezes at 0 ∘ C . x = _________. (Nearest integer) Given : ( K f ) water = 1.86 K kg mol − 1 density of acetic acid is 1.2 g mL − 1 . molar mass of water = 18 g mol − 1 . molar mass of acetic acid= 60 g mol − 1 . density of water = 1 g cm − 3 Acetic acid dissociates as CH 3 COOH ⇌ CH 3 COO ⊖ + H ⊕",
    "optionsEn": [
      "Value 1",
      "Value 2",
      "Value 3",
      "Value 4"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "To solve the problem, we'll calculate the freezing point depression of the acetic acid solution in water. Calculating Moles of Acetic Acid : Given volume of acetic acid: 5 mL Density of acetic acid: 1.2 g/mL Molar mass of acetic acid: 60 g/mol Mass of acetic acid = Volume × Density = 5 mL × 1.2 g/mL = 6 g Moles of acetic acid = Mass Molar mass = 6 g 60 g/mol = 0.1 mol Calculating Molality : Volume of water: 1 L Density of water: 1 g/cm 3 ⇒ 1 kg/L Hence, mass of water = 1 kg Molality (m) = Moles of solute Mass of solvent (kg) = 0.1 mol 1 kg = 0.1 mol/kg Degree of Dissociation ( α ) : Dissociation constant ( K a ) of acetic acid: 6.25 × 10 − 5 α = √ K a C = √ 6.25 × 10 − 5 0.1 = √ 6.25 × 10 − 4 = 25 × 10 − 3 = 0.025 Van't Hoff factor (i) : Acetic acid partially dissociates into 2 ions (CH 3 COO − and H + ) i = 1 + ( number of ions produced − 1 ) × α = 1 + ( 2 − 1 ) × 0.025 = 1 + 0.025 = 1.025 Freezing Point Depression ( Δ T f ) : Freezing point depression constant ( K f ) for water: 1.86 K kg/mol Δ T f = i × K f × Molality = 1.025 × 1.86 × 0.1 = 0.19065 K Final Freezing Point : Pure water freezes at 0 ∘ C Freezing point of solution = 0 ∘ C − 0.19065 ∘ C = − 0.19065 ∘ C Here, − x × 10 − 2 ∘ C is given. Therefore, x = 19 (nearest integer). So, the final answer is: x = 19",
