    "explanationEn": "To find the angle between F ⃗ and p → , we first need to understand the relationship between force and momentum. The force F ⃗ acting on a particle is related to the rate of change of its linear momentum p → with respect to time, as described by Newton's second law of motion: F ⃗ = d p → d t Given the expression for the momentum p → ( t ) = i ˆ cos ⁡ ( k t ) − j ˆ sin ⁡ ( k t ) , we can find F ⃗ by differentiating p → with respect to t : d p → d t = − k i ˆ sin ⁡ ( k t ) − k j ˆ cos ⁡ ( k t ) So, F ⃗ = − k i ˆ sin ⁡ ( k t ) − k j ˆ cos ⁡ ( k t ) . Now, to find the angle between F ⃗ and p → , we use the dot product formula: F ⃗ ⋅ p → = | F ⃗ | | p → | cos ⁡ ( θ ) , where θ is the angle between F ⃗ and p → . However, in this case, it's more insightful to see if F ⃗ and p → are orthogonal (at a π 2 angle to each other), because the dot product of two perpendicular vectors is zero. The dot product of F ⃗ and p → is: ( − k i ˆ sin ⁡ ( k t ) − k j ˆ cos ⁡ ( k t ) ) ⋅ ( i ˆ cos ⁡ ( k t ) − j ˆ sin ⁡ ( k t ) ) = − k sin ⁡ ( k t ) cos ⁡ ( k t ) + k cos ⁡ ( k t ) sin ⁡ ( k t ) = 0 The result is zero, indicating that the angle between F ⃗ and p → is indeed π 2 . Therefore, the correct option is: Option A π 2 .",
