    "correctOptionIndex": 1,
    "explanationEn": "The electromotive force (emf) of a hydrogen electrode can be derived from the Nernst equation, which for the hydrogen half-cell reaction H 2 ( g ) → 2 H + ( aq ) + 2 e − is given by: E = E ∘ + R T 2 F ln ⁡ ( [ H + ] 2 P H 2 ) where: E is the electrode potential. E ∘ is the standard electrode potential, which is 0 V for the hydrogen electrode. R is the gas constant, 8.314 J ⋅ mol − 1 ⋅ K − 1 . T is the temperature in Kelvin: 25 ∘ C = 298 K . F is the Faraday constant, 96485 C ⋅ mol − 1 . [ H + ] is the concentration of hydrogen ions. P H 2 is the pressure of hydrogen gas. In pure water at 25 ∘ C , [ H + ] = 10 − 7 M . To make the emf zero, we set E to 0 in the Nernst equation: 0 = 0 + 8.314 × 298 2 × 96485 ln ⁡ ( ( 10 − 7 ) 2 P H 2 ) Simplifying the constants: 8.314 × 298 2 × 96485 ≈ 0.0128 Thus, the equation becomes: 0 = 0.0128 ln ⁡ ( ( 10 − 7 ) 2 P H 2 ) Since ln term must be zero for this equation to hold true (as 0 divided by any number is still 0), the expression inside the logarithm must equal 1: ( 10 − 7 ) 2 P H 2 = 1 Simplifying this gives: ( 10 − 7 ) 2 = P H 2 10 − 14 = P H 2 Therefore, the required pressure of H 2 to make the emf of the hydrogen electrode zero in pure water at 25 ∘ C is 10 − 14 bar. The correct answer is Option B: 10 − 14 .",
