    "explanationEn": "Initial Conditions: Consider a solid disc of mass M and radius R rolling without slipping on a horizontal surface. The center of mass of the disc has an initial speed v . Kinetic Energy of the Rolling Disc: For an object that rolls without slipping, its total kinetic energy K is the sum of its translational kinetic energy and its rotational kinetic energy. Translational Kinetic Energy: K trans = 1 2 M v 2 . Rotational Kinetic Energy: The moment of inertia I of a solid disc about its center is: I = 1 2 M R 2 . Since the disc rolls without slipping, the angular velocity ω is related to the linear speed by: v = ω R ⟹ ω = v R . Therefore, the rotational kinetic energy is: K rot = 1 2 I ω 2 = 1 2 ( 1 2 M R 2 ) ( v 2 R 2 ) = 1 4 M v 2 . Total Initial Kinetic Energy: Summing these gives: K total = K trans + K rot = 1 2 M v 2 + 1 4 M v 2 = 3 4 M v 2 . Motion on the Smooth Incline: The incline is smooth, so there is no friction on it. Gravity acts at the centre of mass and the normal force passes through the centre of mass, so there is no torque about the centre of mass and the angular velocity ω = v R stays constant. The rotational kinetic energy 1 4 M v 2 therefore does not change: at the maximum height the disc is still spinning. Only the translational kinetic energy is converted into gravitational potential energy: 1 2 M v 2 = M g h . Thus, h = 1 2 v 2 g . Final Answer: 1 2 v 2 g This corresponds to Option D .",
