    "explanationEn": "To calculate the amount of electric charge Q that crosses through a section of the wire over a period of 20 seconds, given the current varies with time as I = I 0 + β t , where I 0 = 20 A (initial current) and β = 3 A/s (rate of change of current with time), we use the concept of integration from calculus because the current is not constant but changes linearly with time. The electric charge Q is the integral of current I over the time interval from 0 to 20 seconds. The formula for Q is given by: Q = ∫ T 0 I ( t ) d t Where I ( t ) = I 0 + β t and T = 20 s. Substituting the given values: Q = ∫ 20 0 ( 20 + 3 t ) d t This integral can be solved in two parts: 1. The integral of the constant term 20 : ∫ 20 d t = 20 t 2. The integral of the linear term 3 t : ∫ 3 t d t = 3 2 t 2 Thus, combining these and evaluating from 0 to 20 seconds: Q = [ 20 t + 3 2 t 2 ] 20 0 Q = [ 20 ( 20 ) + 3 2 ( 20 ) 2 ] − [ 20 ( 0 ) + 3 2 ( 0 ) 2 ] Q = 400 + 600 = 1000 C Therefore, the amount of electric charge that crosses through a section of the wire in 20 seconds is 1000 C.",
