    "explanationEn": "Given quadratic equation: x 2 + √ 6 x + 3 = 0 We can find the roots using the quadratic formula: x = − b ± √ b 2 − 4 a c 2 a Here, a = 1 , b = √ 6 , and c = 3 . Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we have: x = − √ 6 ± √ ( √ 6 ) 2 − 4 ( 1 ) ( 3 ) 2 ( 1 ) Simplifying further : x = − √ 6 ± √ 6 − 12 2 x = − √ 6 ± √ − 6 2 Since the discriminant is negative, the roots are complex numbers. We can express them using the imaginary unit i : x = − √ 6 ± √ 6 i 2 ⇒ x = − 1 2 √ 6 ± 1 2 √ 6 i ∴ α , β = √ 3 e ± 3 π i 4 . The required expression can be rewritten in terms of the argument of the exponential form of the roots, which simplifies the calculation: = ( √ 3 ) 23 ( 2 cos ⁡ 69 π 4 ) + ( √ 3 ) 14 ( 2 cos ⁡ 42 π 4 ) ( √ 3 ) 15 ( 2 cos ⁡ 45 π 4 ) + ( √ 3 ) 10 ( 2 cos ⁡ 30 π 4 ) Here, the exponential power of √ 3 in the numerator is larger by 8 compared to the denominator, so we can divide the numerator and denominator by ( √ 3 ) 8 = 81 to simplify: = ( √ 3 ) 15 ( 2 cos ⁡ 69 π 4 ) + ( √ 3 ) 6 ( 2 cos ⁡ 42 π 4 ) ( √ 3 ) 7 ( 2 cos ⁡ 45 π 4 ) + ( √ 3 ) 2 ( 2 cos ⁡ 30 π 4 ) Since the cosine function has a period of 2 π , we can reduce the arguments of the cosine function in the numerator and denominator. We have 69 π / 4 = π / 4 + 17 π = π / 4 , 42 π / 4 = 2 π / 4 + 10 π = π / 2 , 45 π / 4 = π / 4 + 11 π = π / 4 , and 30 π / 4 = 2 π / 4 + 7 π = π / 2 . Therefore, the required expression simplifies to : = ( √ 3 ) 15 ( 2 cos ⁡ π 4 ) + ( √ 3 ) 6 ( 2 cos ⁡ π 2 ) ( √ 3 ) 7 ( 2 cos ⁡ π 4 ) + ( √ 3 ) 2 ( 2 cos ⁡ π 2 ) = ( √ 3 ) 15 √ 2 + ( √ 3 ) 6 ⋅ 0 ( √ 3 ) 7 √ 2 + ( √ 3 ) 2 ⋅ 0 = ( √ 3 ) 15 √ 2 ( √ 3 ) 7 √ 2 = ( √ 3 ) 15 − 7 = ( √ 3 ) 8 = 81.",
